    "answer": {
      "short": "$y'=3(x-2)^2+6>0$ for all real $x$.\n\nSince the squared term is non-negative, the derivative is at least $6$, so the function is strictly increasing.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y'&=3x^2-12x+18\\\\y'&=3(x^2-4x+4)+6\\\\y'&=3(x-2)^2+6\\end{align*}$$ Since $(x-2)^2\\ge 0$ for all real $x$, $y'\\ge 6>0$ everywhere. Therefore the function is strictly increasing.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
